Here's a simple and practical script:

```powershell
# Check and restart a Windows service
# Change 'Spooler' to whatever service you want to monitor

$serviceName = 'Spooler'
$service = Get-Service -Name $serviceName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

if ($null -eq $service) {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' not found on this computer." -ForegroundColor Red
    exit 1
}

if ($service.Status -ne 'Running') {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' is $($service.Status). Attempting restart..." -ForegroundColor Yellow
    try {
        Restart-Service -Name $serviceName -Force
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 5
        $service.Refresh()
        if ($service.Status -eq 'Running') {
            Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' restarted successfully." -ForegroundColor Green
        } else {
            Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' failed to start. Status: $($service.Status)" -ForegroundColor Red
        }
    } catch {
        Write-Host "Error restarting service: $_" -ForegroundColor Red
    }
} else {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' is already running." -ForegroundColor Green
}
```

Save this as `check-service.ps1`. Run it with: `.\check-service.ps1`

To run it on a schedule, use Task Scheduler: Create a task > trigger it every 5 minutes > Action: Start a program > `powershell.exe` with arguments `-File C:\scripts\check-service.ps1`.

⚠ Make sure the account running the scheduled task has permission to restart services (usually needs to be a local admin or SYSTEM).
